# Named local.env.example (not .env.local.example) — same device-bridge
# write-guard workaround as every other node's local.env.example (anything
# matching *.env.local* gets blocked, even a harmless placeholder).
#
# Copy this file to .env.local (already covered by this repo's
# `*.env.local` .gitignore rule — never commit the real one) and fill in
# real values. Shared by every app in this stack via ./compose.sh and
# render-configs.sh.
#
# Deliberately minimal, same reasoning as mochaPot's local.env.example
# when it was first scaffolded — only what apps here actually need.
# No DOMAIN here on purpose (unlike every other node's local.env.example):
# nothing on roastery is ever routed through Traefik or reached by
# hostname — Jellyfin and immich-ml are both reached by raw IP:port
# (LAN and tailnet respectively). Add it later if that ever changes;
# don't carry it forward speculatively the way other nodes did.
#
# Note on "tailnet-only, no public exposure" (initiation.txt Risk R9/R10):
# that's about never exposing roastery to the *internet* (Vanguard
# anti-cheat widens this machine's attack surface if anything here is
# internet-reachable) — it does NOT mean every app here is tailnet-only.
# immich-ml is tailnet-bound because it has zero authentication of its
# own; Jellyfin has real login, so it's reachable on the normal LAN like
# every other node's apps, same as mochaPot's original Jellyfin plan —
# just never port-forwarded past the router.

TZ=Asia/Kolkata

# This machine's own Headscale tailnet IP (the 100.64.0.0/10 address
# Tailscale assigns once enrolled) — NOT roastery's LAN IP. Needed so
# immich-ml/docker-compose.yml can bind port 3003 to this address
# specifically rather than 0.0.0.0: Immich's own docs state the ML
# container "has no security measures whatsoever" (no auth at all), so
# binding it to the tailnet interface — reachable only from other
# tailnet members (mochaPot), never from the LAN or the internet — is
# the mitigation, not optional hardening.
#
# Get this value by enrolling roastery in the fleet's Headscale tailnet
# first (this has to happen on roastery itself, in a real Windows/WSL2
# terminal — nothing here can do it):
#   1. On sieve:
#        sudo docker exec headscale headscale preauthkeys create \
#          --user barista --expiration 1h
#   2. On roastery, in WSL2, with Tailscale installed:
#        sudo tailscale up --login-server=https://headscale.${DOMAIN} \
#          --authkey=<key from step 1>
#   3. Then:
#        tailscale ip -4
#      That output is this value.
# See stacks/roastery/README.md's "Enroll roastery in Headscale" section
# for the full walkthrough, and stacks/sieve/README.md's
# headscale-bootstrap.sh section for the `barista` user this reuses
# (already created 2026-08-30 — no need to create it again).
ROASTERY_TAILNET_IP=REPLACE_ME

# Added 2026-09-05 for jellyfin/docker-compose.yml. The WSL2 path to your
# existing media library -- e.g. if it's on your D: drive under
# D:\Media, that's /mnt/d/Media from inside WSL2. Deliberately not
# guessed: this is real personal data placement on your own machine, not
# something a fleet convention can supply. Local/on-roastery is the
# starting choice (same stopgap mochaPot's original Jellyfin plan used —
# cellar's NFS export isn't set up yet, see stacks/cellar/README.md's
# smb/ section) — swap this for an NFS mount once that exists, if you'd
# rather centralize media there instead of keeping it local to roastery.
ROASTERY_MEDIA_PATH=REPLACE_ME
